<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; border: 2px solid #1a5276;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 28px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Life Adventures – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Life Adventures</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Big Book!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the main plot of simple stories told with visual support. Interactive: Demonstrate comprehension of a variety of classroom commands.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow the main idea of a simple story and identify familiar vocabulary when visuals are provided (e.g., a story about a small red pencil and a big blue book going to school, with students identifying them as the teacher points to the corresponding pictures while asking, "What size is the pencil?"). Can understand and respond appropriately to basic classroom commands (e.g., "Please sit down." or "Open your book."). Can participate in brief exchanges using simple, familiar classroom phrases (e.g., responding to "What color is the bag?" with "It's brown.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification: Activities to activate prior knowledge, introduce the theme, and model new language in a clear, accessible way for young learners, ensuring understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game using real classroom objects and simple commands related to size and color. For example: "Simon says, 'Touch a big book!'" (Teacher holds up a big book). "Simon says, 'Point to a small pencil!'" (Teacher holds up a small pencil). "Simon says, 'Find a red bag!'" (Teacher points to a red bag). This activates vocabulary and involves TPR.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up realia (a book, a pencil, a bag) and large flashcards showing the items in different colors and sizes. Teacher models new vocabulary with clear pronunciation and actions: "This is a <b>book</b>. It's a <b>big</b>, <b>blue</b> book." (Pronouncing /b/ in 'book' clearly). "This is a <b>pencil</b>. It's a <b>small</b>, <b>red</b> pencil." (Pronouncing /p/ in 'pencil' and /s/ in 'small' clearly). "This is a <b>bag</b>. It's a <b>brown</b> bag." The teacher uses gestures to indicate 'big' and 'small' and points to the object and color. The teacher also models polite classroom commands like "Please sit down" and "Open your book."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with simple questions and visual cues, encouraging students to point or give single-word responses.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a big book) "Is this a book or a pencil?" (Expected: "Book" or pointing).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a small pencil) "Is the pencil big or small?" (Expected: "Small" or pointing to the small object).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a blue bag) "What color is the bag?" (Expected: "Blue" or pointing to the color blue).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Life Adventures." Students are exposed to the target language through authentic or semi-authentic materials.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple illustrated story (e.g., a big picture book or large flashcards forming a sequence) about a small red pencil and a big blue book going to school together. The story uses repetitive language and clear visuals. For example: "This is a small red pencil. Hello, Pencil! This is a big blue book. Hello, Book! The small red pencil is on the big blue book. They go to school!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the main plot of simple stories told with visual support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening for gist and specific details):</b> The teacher reads the story clearly and slowly, pointing to the corresponding pictures.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> After the first reading, the teacher asks: "Who is in the story? A pencil and a book? Yes or No?" (Students respond with 'Yes' or thumbs up/down).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identifying Objects:</b> The teacher reads the story again, pausing at key points. "This is a small red pencil." (Teacher points to the pencil picture). "Is this the <b>small</b> pencil? Yes/No?" (Students respond). "And this is a <b>big</b> blue book." (Teacher points to the book picture). "What color is the book?" (Students point to blue or say "Blue").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual Check):</b> The teacher makes simple statements based on the story, and students signal True (thumbs up) or False (thumbs down) while looking at the pictures. For example: "The pencil is big. True or False?" (Story says "small red pencil"). "The book is blue. True or False?" (Story says "big blue book"). "The pencil is on the book. True or False?" (Students check the picture).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening. Students engage in controlled and semi-controlled activities to reinforce listening comprehension of vocabulary and grammar from the presentation stage.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension of adjectives (big, small, red, blue, brown), nouns (book, pencil, bag), demonstratives (this, that), prepositions (on, in), Present Simple ("That's a..."), and WH-questions ("What size is the book?"). Pronunciation focus: /b/, /s/, /p/ sounds. Pragmatic: Responding to questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Point (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher displays a set of flashcards or realia (e.g., a big blue book, a small red pencil, a brown bag, a small yellow crayon). The teacher says a phrase, and students listen and point to the correct item. E.g., "Point to the big blue book." "Point to the small red pencil." "Point to the brown bag." "Point to the small yellow crayon." The teacher can also ask, "What size is the book?" (Students point to big/small or say "Big").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Color (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with outlines of classroom objects (e.g., a book, a pencil, a bag) and simple labels. The teacher gives instructions: "Listen carefully! Color the big book blue. Color the small pencil red. Color the bag brown." Students listen and color according to the instructions. This reinforces listening for color and size adjectives.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen and Place (Pair/Group Interaction):</b> In small groups or pairs, students have realia or cut-out pictures of a few classroom objects (book, pencil, bag) and a small "desk" drawing. The teacher gives commands: "Please put the small pencil on the big book." "Put the big book in the bag." Students listen and perform the action. The teacher circulates and asks, "Is the pencil on the book?" (Students point or nod). This activity also implicitly models polite expressions like "Can you please pass me the blue pencil?" (teacher modeling while circulating).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Life Adventures." Focus is on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion, primarily through listening and responding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Classroom Map!" - A listening-based arrangement activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a large piece of paper representing a classroom floor (e.g., with outlines for a desk and chair) and pre-cut out pictures of classroom objects (e.g., a big blue book, a small red pencil, a brown bag, a green ruler, a yellow eraser, a white paper).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives a series of sequential commands, slowly and clearly, for students to arrange their cut-out objects on their "classroom map."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples: "Please put the <b>big blue book on</b> the <b>desk</b>." "Put the <b>small red pencil next to</b> the <b>book</b>." "Put the <b>brown bag under</b> the <b>chair</b>." "Now, put the <b>green ruler in</b> the <b>bag</b>." "And the <b>yellow eraser on</b> the <b>desk</b>, <b>next to</b> the <b>pencil</b>."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and place the objects accordingly. This requires sustained listening to commands with adjectives and prepositions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on listening comprehension leading to a correct physical arrangement, connecting to the theme "That's a Big Book!" and classroom objects.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to gauge students' listening comprehension and application of the target language. Includes student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Classroom Map!" activity. The teacher walks around, observes individual students' arrangements, and checks for correct placement based on listening commands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' final arrangement of objects on their "My Classroom Map!" based on the teacher's auditory instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly identifies familiar vocabulary (e.g., book, pencil, bag, desk, chair).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows simple commands involving adjectives (big, small, color adjectives).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Understands and applies prepositions (on, in, under, next to).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds appropriately to commands by performing the action.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket by drawing a happy or sad face next to two statements or by giving a thumbs up/down:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand words like 'big' and 'small'."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can follow simple instructions about classroom objects."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reflecting on the learning process for both students and the teacher, and preparing for the next lesson in the sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about school things did you hear today?" (Students can point to flashcards or realia).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little hard to understand the story and the commands?" (Students can show 'easy' or 'a little hard' with a hand gesture or simple word).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging students and developing listening comprehension at this level? (e.g., TPR, visual aids, story repetition).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which vocabulary items or grammatical structures (e.g., prepositions 'on/in') were particularly challenging for students to grasp through listening? What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to reinforce these?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did students respond to the classroom commands and story comprehension tasks?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a simple text using the core vocabulary introduced today (book, pencil, bag, big, small, red, blue, brown, on, in). They will recognize these words in print, reinforcing the concepts learned through listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>